WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:06.920
Hey everyone, Jared here.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.440
Today we’ve got a special episode about
a special show: Rick and Morty.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.020
Every episode is chock full of references
to sci-fi, horror, and fantasy --

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:25.280
from the aliens, to the premises, to the gadgets...the
list is endless.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.140
While many of these references are throwaway
jokes or simple homage,

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.980
others can be quite thought provoking and insightful.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.720
By observing how Rick and Morty inverts common
cliches and tropes,

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:41.240
we can better understand one of the smartest shows on television — and why we love it so much.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.760
Welcome to this special Wisecrack Edition
on references in Rick and Morty.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.100
By the way, this is a slightly different format from how we've done things in the past,

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.780
so if you like it...let us know what you think in the comments!

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.280
In the episode “Something Ricked This Way Comes,”

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.700
Rick and Morty uses the arrival of Satan
to turn the idea of “deal with the devil” on its head.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.980
The episode title comes from Ray Bradbury’s
“Something Wicked This Way Comes,”

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:11.280
a novel about some weird carnies with a magical carousel that enables people to become younger or older at will.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.500
Of course, there’s a catch, and anybody who benefits from this magic also becomes beholden to the evil of the carnival.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.840
Making deals with creepy strangers is also
a theme in Stephen King’s novel “Needful Things,”

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.960
which, surprise, is the name of
the Devil’s shop of oddities in Rick and Morty.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.920
In the book, people are tricked into buying
garbage that appears to be rare or valuable at a very low cost.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:33.540
The catch?

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:34.940
In exchange for a great deal,

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:39.940
you must play a few pranks on your neighbors that end up throwing the town into a kind of civil war.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:43.300
In Rick and Morty, the Devil opens up a shop
where the items are free,

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.060
but also creatively ruin whatever benefit one hopes to receive.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.980
Rick gets a magic microscope that will make
him too stupid to use it.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:55.860
Morty’s math teacher gets an aftershave
that attracts women but also makes him impotent.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.500
Stories about making a deal with the devil
often reflect the hidden consequences

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:04.860
that result from trying to thwart the natural order of things in order to fulfill your selfish desires.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:09.720
In good Rick and Morty fashion, the show takes
this moral refrain and throws it in the toilet.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.600
In Rick’s version of the story, you can
have everything you want -- because science.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:31.740
No need to sell your soul or deal with a debilitating
curse to get what you want.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.700
With science, there are no mystical repercussions
for messing with the perceived order of things,

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.980
You can have your cake and eat it too.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:53.180
Just as science demystifies religion by exposing magic and miracles as either sleight of hand or mere statistical coincidence,

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.600
Rick demystifies the “deal with the devil” trope as nothing more than a paranoid fairy tale.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:12.080
What these stories often portray as “too good to be true,” is usually just shorthand for “don’t circumvent God’s rules,"

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.340
which is the preferred pastime of every scientist.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.700
With science, Rick renders the devil obsolete.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.800
That’s the real lesson here: evil isn’t
what should horrify us anymore.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.820
Karmic retribution is kind of calming: evil-doers
will eventually face cosmic justice.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.280
A world without this is scary in its own right.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.980
And while the devil may be terrifying, if
you want to find something really scary,

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.320
you need to look beyond evil and take a look at
pure, disinterested science.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.540
Or, as the devil puts it:

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.460
In “Auto Erotic Assimilation,”

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:54.660
Rick and Morty takes popular fears of conformity to criticize our basic values and explore Rick’s inner psyche.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.880
In the episode, Rick runs into his ex-girlfriend
“Unity,”

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:03.700
who assimilates alien races into her hivemind-collective by puking into their mouths.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.700
It’s a reference to the 1956 film “Invasion
of the Body Snatchers,”

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:13.640
although the idea of aliens turning us into some sort of zombie collective has become a familiar trope in science fiction.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:19.800
Invasion of the Body Snatchers is about an alien assault that replaces people with creepy emotionless replicas of themselves,

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.140
similar to Unity’s assimilated subjects.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:28.400
Invasion of the Body Snatchers has been read by many film critics as a commentary on the then-impending threat of the Soviet Union

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.240
and the loss of individualism under Communist rule.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.880
One way Rick and Morty inverts the body snatchers
story is by demonstrating that

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.900
the freedom we associate with individualism can actually kind of suck.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:52.320
Whereas The Invasion of the Body Snatchers projects an anxiety about Communism destroying individualism,

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.380
Rick and Morty shows the very-not-fictional horrors of our own freedoms.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:07.600
The denizens of Unity’s planet recall their
previous lives as sex offenders and drug addicts,

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.840
and their liberation from the collective whole
sparks a nipple-driven race war.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:20.100
This tension between individual freedom and
a collective existence further develops Rick’s character.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:25.140
After all, being a part of a family, or in
a relationship,

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:29.040
is really about compromising your individual freedoms for the good of the group.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:41.200
Throughout the entire series, Rick’s rampant
egotism impedes his relationship with his family.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:48.480
Likewise, in Auto Erotic Assimilation, Rick’s egotism not only destroys his relationship with Unity, but her entire society.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:57.320
And of course, in Unity’s letter to Rick, it’s actually Rick who makes Unity lose her sense of self in their relationship.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:09.180
As a bonus, the show on TV is a recreation
of Community, Dan Harmon’s show before Rick and Morty.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.660
It’s main character, Jeff Winger, is, like
Rick,

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:23.300
also an egotistical asshole trying to make sense of what it means to be around people who care about him.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:32.800
The 1974 film Zardoz is one of the more obscure
references to play a prominent role in the show.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.820
If you've been exposed to Zardoz, it’s likely through an internet meme’d Sean Connery wearing uh, this,

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:46.180
or a giant floating head
vomiting up guns, saying, “The gun is good, the penis is evil."

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:49.400
Rick and Morty is, in some ways, thematically
similar to Zardoz,

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.760
and uses the film as a basis for the episode “Raising Gazorpazorp.”

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:58.980
In Zardoz, a giant-floating-head-god commands
some chosen savages to go murder the rest of the savages.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:04.220
One of these chosen savages, Zed, discovers
his giant-floating-head-god is a lie,

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:09.720
and that it was all some convoluted plan to enslave
the savages in service of some immortal aristocrats.

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:22.020
Raising Gazorpazorp takes the plot and changes
a few details:

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:25.760
the conflict between immortals and savages is replaced by men and women,

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:27.140
the penis is still evil,

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.580
but it’s all precipitated because Morty wants a sex doll.

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:38.440
The Zardoz reference follows up on a general
theme of atheism in the show.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:47.220
Zardoz is a movie about a guy who discovers
his God is a lie and proceeds to kill that God.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:54.240
Rick and Morty is a show reminding us that
either there is no God, or that God isn’t all that great.

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:05.180
The episode “Lawnmower Dog” borrows its
title from the 1992 film “Lawnmower Man,”

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:09.440
a movie about a dumb gardener who is turned
into a crazy genius by Pierce Brosnan.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:14.340
In Lawnmower Dog, Rick makes the family pooch,
Snuffles, smart enough to stop peeing on the rug,

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:18.360
but, by accident, also smart enough to
subjugate the entire human race.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.180
Snuffles rejects his “slave name” in favor
of “Snowball,”

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:34.180
a reference to the pig, and Leon Trotsky stand-in, from George Orwell’s Animal Farm.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:38.060
“Snowball” seems to be a pretty popular
name for revolutionary animals these days,

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:41.540
as the antagonist of “Secret Life of Pets”
also shares the name.

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:44.240
The interspecies war that transpires in Rick
and Morty

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:48.580
is similar to the war waged by the creatures of Animal Farm against their human masters.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:52.380
Whereas the farm animals rebel against their
labor being exploited by lazy humans,

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.260
Snuffles rebels against the lazy life of pethood.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:02.920
In the book, the rebellious animals slowly
turn into the farmers that they had fought against.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:09.780
The final scene shows pigs standing on two feet and wearing clothes, completing the transformation into their former overlords.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.960
In Rick and Morty, with the help of Inception,

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:26.120
a dystopian nightmare is averted when Snowball realizes he’s slowly turning into everything he hated about humanity.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:37.480
The episode also makes a few references to
“Dogworld,” a series concept developed by show co-creator Justin Roiland.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:42.580
It features a family who gets transported
to an alternate dimension where the world is run by dogs.

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:47.820
The pet human is named “Ruffles,” which
Rick mistakenly calls Snuffles in Lawnmower Dog.

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:53.500
Also, at the end of the episode, when the
dogs leave to populate a new dimension,

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:57.640
Rick makes a smooth pitch for the development of
“Dogworld” as its own show.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:13.300
There are too many references to fairy tales,
science fiction, and horror to list,

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:17.240
but many of them further this idea of disillusionment central to the show.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.460
When the show isn’t deconstructing mysticism
with hard science,

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:30.380
it’s deconstructing fantasies by subjecting them to the mundanity of everyday life.

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:33.580
Meeseeks and Destroy is the most overt example of
this.

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:36.720
Morty brings Rick on a cliche fairytale adventure:

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.840
raising money for a poor village by playing out the story from Jack and the Beanstalk.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:45.060
To summarize the fairy tale, a boy robs a
giant, unprovoked, kills him,

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:50.540
and lives his life happily ever after with his stolen riches
without suffering any consequences.

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:56.640
The show “normalizes” the fairy tale by subjecting it to a level of realism usually omitted from children’s stories —

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:01.180
dispelling the cheery optimism that comforted you as a child.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:04.640
Instead of ending the story with some bullshit
about happily ever after,

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:10.540
Morty is put on trial for his senseless murder and confronted with the emotionally devastated widow of the giant.

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:13.590
Also, the village’s king turns out to be
sexual predator.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:19.460
In another episode, we’re introduced to Scary Terry,

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:23.420
a legally safe knockoff of Freddy Krueger from “A Nightmare on Elm Street.”

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:28.240
While Scary Terry may seem terrifying, with
his ability to murder you in your dreams and all,

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.240
his life is utterly banal.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:34.280
The show dispels the horror of Freddy Kreuger with the same kind of normalization.

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:38.980
The tormenter of dreams, as it turns out, has
the same shitty home life as the rest of us,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:42.940
and is scared of the same lame dreams.

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:47.040
Anatomy Park is a reference to both Jurassic
Park and Fantastic Voyage.

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:48.980
Rick and Morty shows that the human body

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:54.000
— which is often portrayed by movies like Fantastic Voyage as a wonder of the natural world —

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:59.180
is just a gonorrhea infested nightmare.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:06.900
If there’s one humorous device that Rick and Morty uses more than any other, it’s robbing fantasy of its magic.

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.980
It trivializes everything.

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:15.140
Not only fairy tales, but family, love, and
even life.

